{
  "_about": "Extension services' planting calendars, loaded where one is published for a town's region (src/lib/crop-calendar.ts extensionRegion). Added after the Day 4 audit (13 Nov 2026): the monthly-mean model inverted the subtropical season in South Florida (warm crops sown through the summer and refused Nov-Jan; lettuce, radish, spinach, peas and potatoes cut or missing), gave Orlando and Tampa summer warm-crop windows UF/IFAS does not give, sowed Las Vegas into November (UNR: Aug 10 to Oct 10), ran Phoenix past the University of Arizona's cut-offs, sowed Sacramento a month late and gave San Diego and Los Angeles all-year or summer sowings. Where a region is loaded, its calendar decides the sowing (or planting-out) windows for the months in `covers`: a crop in `crops` is sown on the source's dates, and with `others` \"none\" a crop the source does not list is not sown in those months. Months outside `covers` keep the model. Each `sow` span is [first day, last day] as MM-DD (a span may run over New Year); `printed` is the source's own cell. Harvests are still worked out from each crop's days to maturity. `towns` are the site's towns inside the region, each placed by the boundary the source gives (see `placed`).",
  "dateWords": "Month names alone cover the whole month (Feb ends on the 28th). 'Early <month>' is the 1st to the 10th; a window that starts 'Mid <month>' starts on the 15th and one that starts 'Late <month>' on the 21st; 'Mid July' on its own is the 11th to the 20th; 'end of <month>' is from the 21st. The University of Arizona calendar marks half-month columns headed with their first day (Jan 1, Jan 15 ...): a marked column is read as that half month, so 'Aug 15 - Dec 15' runs from Aug 15 to Dec 31.",
