  "unread": {
    "ua-pima": "University of Arizona, Pima County Planting Guide (https://www.extension.arizona.edu/sites/default/files/2025-01/Pima-County-Vegetable-Planting-and-Harvesting-Guide-Adobe-PDF-1.pdf, opened 13 Nov 2026): the half-month marks cannot be matched to their columns in the PDF's text. Tucson keeps the model.",
    "ucmg-santa-clara": "UC Master Gardeners of Santa Clara County, Vegetable Planting Chart (https://ucanr.edu/sites/mgscc2016/garden-help/vegetables/vegetable-planting-chart/, opened 13 Nov 2026): the chart is a graphic with no text; the printable chart (mastergardeners.org/vpc/chartonly.html) is closed to readers by robots.txt. San Jose keeps the model.",
    "ucmg-santa-barbara": "No UC Master Gardeners of Santa Barbara County planting calendar found (search, 13 Nov 2026). Santa Barbara keeps the model.",
    "nmsu": "NMSU Circular 457 and 457-B (https://pubs.nmsu.edu/_circulars/CR457/, https://pubs.nmsu.edu/_circulars/CR457B/, revised Jan 2026, opened 13 Nov 2026) time planting from the last frost and give no dated windows for Albuquerque. Albuquerque keeps the model.",
    "uidaho": "University of Idaho BUL-965, Spring Vegetable Planting Guide for Idaho (2020, https://www.uidaho.edu/-/media/UIdaho-Responsive/Files/Extension/publications/bul/bul965.pdf, opened 13 Nov 2026) gives spring dates by zone group (zones 3-4, 5-6, 7), not for Boise, spring only, and the columns of its direct-seeded rows cannot be told apart in the PDF's text. Boise keeps the model.",
    "tamu-eht056": "Texas A&M AgriLife Extension, Fall Vegetable Gardening Guide (EHT-056, Larry Stein and Joe Masabni, 5/14; https://aggie-horticulture.tamu.edu/vegetable/files/2013/09/EHT-056.pdf and https://agrilifeextension.tamu.edu/browse/featured-solutions/gardening-landscaping/fall-vegetable-gardening-guide-for-texas, opened 14 Nov 2026): Table 1 gives one 'average planting date' per crop and region, not a window, and its regions are drawn only on a map image (the page's alt text: 'five gardening regions, which progress numerically from north to south'); no text puts Austin or San Antonio in Region III or IV. Not loaded; San Antonio takes the Bexar County Master Gardeners' calendar.",
    "travis-county": "Texas A&M AgriLife Extension, Travis County, Monthly Gardening Calendar for Austin Area (https://travis-tx.tamu.edu/?p=11009, last modified 4 Feb 2022, opened 14 Nov 2026): the overview page links twelve monthly pages and gives no dated windows itself; the monthly pages were not read. Austin keeps the model."
  },
